{"name":"Morning dress","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Morning_Dress_LGE.jpg/960px-Morning_Dress_LGE.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morning_Dress_LGE.jpg","caption":"Morning Dress LGE","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["Morning dress is the traditional formal daytime dress code: a single-breasted morning coat with curved tails, a waistcoat, striped or checked trousers, a white shirt, and polished black shoes. Its appearance payoff is a structured, elongated look that signals formality.","It developed from riding coats in the 19th century and replaced the frock coat as standard daytime full dress in the Edwardian era. Today it is seen at British and Commonwealth weddings, Royal Ascot, and some official occasions."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["Men wear the coat and waistcoat (matching or contrasting), striped trousers with braces and no turn-ups, a white double-cuff shirt with tie or cravat, black Oxford shoes, and optionally a top hat, gloves and boutonniere. Variants include a matching morning suit and all-grey morning grey."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["There are no health risks. Practical issues are etiquette: Royal Ascot banned cravats in 2012 and bow ties in 2019 in the Royal Enclosure, and the code is not for events starting after 18:00."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Morning_Dress_LGE.jpg/960px-Morning_Dress_LGE.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a7/Morning_Dress_LGE.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Morning Dress LGE","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","creator":"Loris Gerber Loris_85","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File